## Final Revision

1. JVM executes Java bytecode.
2. JRE refers to the runtime environment.
3. JDK provides Java development tools.
4. `javac` compiles Java source code into bytecode.
5. The JVM can interpret bytecode and use JIT compilation.
6. Java achieves platform independence through compatible JVM implementations.
7. Java SE provides core Java functionality.
8. Java EE evolved into Jakarta EE.
9. Java ME targets certain embedded and resource-constrained environments.
10. The standard entry point of a typical Java application is the `main()` method.
